      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "HISTORY",
          "content": "A 46-year-old woman presented for an emergency admission because of colic-like upper abdominal pain and markedly impaired general condition. Eight days before admission she had started and continued to take pantoprazole because of symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INVESTIGATIONS",
          "content": "Clinical examination and laboratory tests indicated abnormal liver functions suggesting hepatitis. Serology largely excluded an infectious, autoimmunological or metabolic cause. Duplex sonography gave no evidence of bile obstruction or Budd-Chiari syndrome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DIAGNOSIS AND COURSE",
          "content": "The patient's condition and laboratory tests after the drug had been discontinued gradually improved on symptomatic treatment, indicating pantoprazole-induced hepatitis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Intake of proton pump inhibitors is a rare cause but should be considered in the differential diagnosis of hepatitis of uncertain etiology."
        }
      ]},
